LWJGL 3 (lwjgl, lwjgl-glfw, lwjgl-jemalloc, lwjgl-openal, lwjgl-opengl,
lwjgl-stb)
    Copyright (c) 2012-2024 Lightweight Java Game Library
    License: BSD 3-Clause
    https://www.lwjgl.org/

    Ships precompiled natives for Windows, macOS and Linux. Those natives embed
    further upstream projects under their own licenses, principally GLFW
    (zlib/libpng), OpenAL Soft (LGPL 2.1), jemalloc (BSD 2-Clause) and
    stb (MIT / public domain). See https://www.lwjgl.org/license.

    Gamepad support is GLFW's, through lwjgl-glfw, and it carries one piece of
    third-party DATA worth naming separately from the code above:

        SDL_GameControllerDB gamepad mappings
            Author:   Sam Lantinga / the SDL project and contributors
            Source:   https://github.com/mdqinc/SDL_GameControllerDB (upstream),
                      vendored into GLFW as src/mappings.h
            License:  zlib — the same license as GLFW itself, which is why it
                      needs no separate grant here

    That table is what makes glfwGetGamepadState report a standardised
    Xbox-shaped layout for a DualShock, a Switch Pro pad or a third-party clone,
    so DesktopBindings can name "button A" once instead of once per brand.

    NO GAMEPAD LIBRARY IS SHIPPED, and that is a decision rather than an
    omission. libGDX's gdx-controllers extension is the obvious route; its
    desktop backend is Jamepad and therefore SDL, which would add several
    megabytes of precompiled native library per platform and a second license
    chain to this file — to reach the same mapping database GLFW already has
    loaded in the same process. OpenFPS calls GLFW's copy. See GlfwGamepad.

    The Android build needs no equivalent: gamepad buttons arrive as ordinary
    key events and stick axes as MotionEvent axes, both from the platform. See
    AndroidInputPort.

